<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Debug-profile entitlements (Runner / container app).

  IMPORTANT: hardened-runtime relaxations (allow-jit,
  allow-unsigned-executable-memory, disable-library-validation) are
  INTENTIONALLY ABSENT — exactly the same constraint as Release.entitlements.

  Why: the dev portal App ID `art.arcane.ArcaneDispatch` has the Network
  Extension capability enabled. Apple's auto-issued provisioning profile
  therefore carries `com.apple.developer.system-extension.install`, and
  AMFI's rule "Hardened Runtime relaxation entitlements disallowed on
  System Extensions" applies to ANY binary launched under that profile —
  including this Runner. Claiming `allow-jit` etc. causes the kernel to
  refuse the launch with `POSIX 163 / Launchd job spawn failed`, which is
  what IntelliJ's "Run" / "Debug" buttons surfaced as
  `RBSRequestErrorDomain Code=5 Launch failed.`.

  Trade-off: Dart's normal debug-mode JIT cannot run without these
  entitlements. The app now launches successfully from IntelliJ /
  flutter run, but use `--profile` for development:

      flutter run -d macos --profile

  Profile mode AOT-compiles Dart (no JIT needed), keeps DevTools /
  observatory, and is otherwise indistinguishable from Debug for everyday
  development. Hot-reload won't work in --profile, but the Tunnel-mode
  feature itself fully works there.

  To regain Debug-mode hot-reload (optional, not required for the app to
  function), the dev portal App ID for `art.arcane.ArcaneDispatch` must
  have the System Extensions capability REMOVED. See README troubleshooting.

  Network-Extension entitlement is REQUIRED — without it
  `NETunnelProviderManager.saveToPreferences` fails before macOS ever
  shows the "ArcaneDispatch wants to add VPN configurations" dialog.

  App Groups (`com.apple.security.application-groups`) is also absent —
  the provisioning profile does not currently grant it. Policy and
  server config are passed to the extension through
  `NETunnelProviderProtocol.providerConfiguration` (see
  `macos/Runner/TunnelManager.swift`).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
	<array>
		<string>packet-tunnel-provider</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
